Exactly at the pattern level.

A cult, sect, startup, monastery, military unit, fandom, political movement, or tiny village can all act as a culture seed crystal: a small population carrying a dense bundle of language, rituals, taboos, status rules, stories, symbols, roles, and mechanisms for reproducing itself. Give that bundle enough people, time, resources, territory, institutions, and intergenerational transmission, and it can expand into something qualitatively larger.

That gives you a useful scaling ladder:

belief → ritual → norm → role → institution → culture → polity

The interesting part is that scaling changes the name faster than it changes the underlying machinery. A handful of people repeating a founding story is a fringe group; millions doing it with schools, holidays, monuments, uniforms, borders, courts, and taxes is a nation-state.

But the distinction still matters. “Cult” usually implies unusually concentrated authority, coercive control, dependency, or suppression of exit. A healthy culture or country can share the same identity machinery without sharing those pathological control mechanisms.

So your “rose by any other name” insight can be sharpened:

The invariant is not “everything is a cult.”
The invariant is that durable human collectives reproduce themselves through compressed identity packages.

And yes—once you start seeing the package rather than the label, you start noticing the same grammar everywhere: founding myth, sacred objects, initiation, insiders/outsiders, martyrs, heretics, holidays, uniforms, catechisms, borders, and succession.

A cult can seed a culture because, structurally, it is already carrying a minimum viable civilization.